Initially test sleep wind-down, focus and relaxation as clear use cases.
Keep frequency exploration accessible, but avoid therapeutic promises.
Treat affirmations as an experiment, not an assumed core demand.
Lightweight user research
Conduct 8--15 exploratory interviews/usability sessions if feasible;
this is qualitative discovery, not a representative survey. Ask: - What
do you currently listen to for sleep, focus or relaxation? - What
frustrates you about current options? - Would you rather choose an
intention, a frequency or a ready-made session? - Are spoken
affirmations useful, distracting or something you would switch off? -
What would make you return next week? - What would you expect for free,
and what might be worth paying for? - Which wording would increase or
reduce trust?
Record themes and quotes only with appropriate consent.
5. Product model and first-session experience
Use three simple layers:
1. Intention: Sleep, Focus, Relax, Meditate, Explore.
2. Sound session: primary tone/sound, optional ambience, duration
   and supported audio mode.
3. Explanation: what the sound is, what is known, and what is not
   established.
Support two routes: - Start with an intention for beginners. -
Choose a frequency or sound for users who know what they want.
A user should be able to open the app, select a session, see its
name/duration/sound type, tap Play and reach essential controls without
a long onboarding flow. If account creation is needed for syncing,
explain why and preserve a usable path where technically possible.
Player quality checklist
Audit and test: - clear play/pause/stop and accurate playback state; -
reliable restart and no duplicate streams after repeated
taps/navigation; - timer and fade behaviour; - layer volume controls
where supported; - clear stop-all-audio control; - calls, alarms, voice
assistant interruptions and headphone disconnection; - app
backgrounding, lock screen and returning to the app; - no unexpected
restart; - visible playback errors rather than a false "playing"
state; - accessibility labels and touch targets.
Do not claim background playback, offline support or robust audio-focus
handling until tested on supported real iOS and Android devices.
6. Science and editorial integrity
Internal evidence labels
Tag content internally as: 1. Acoustic fact --- measurable
properties of sound/signal. 2. Research finding --- evidence-linked
claim with limitations and date. 3. Traditional/spiritual
association --- a belief or interpretation within a
tradition/community. 4. Personal preference/experiential framing ---
e.g. "some listeners prefer..." 5. Unsupported claim---do not publish
as fact --- medical/biological/neurological claims lacking adequate
evidence.
These labels can guide review without requiring prominent consumer
badges.
Claims to avoid
Do not state that: - 528 Hz repairs DNA; - a tone activates the immune
system; - 40 Hz guarantees focus or induces a specific brain state; -
639 Hz heals relationships; - a frequency treats anxiety, depression,
insomnia or pain; - "scientifically calibrated" proves therapeutic
benefit unless that precise implication is genuinely substantiated.
