Technical accuracy: low tones and binaural beats
- Pure 1.5 Hz or 7.83 Hz is below ordinary human hearing. Do not
  describe it as a normally audible phone-speaker tone.
- If using "Schumann-inspired," distinguish an audible sound design
  inspired by the concept from reproducing Earth's electromagnetic
  resonance itself.
- Binaural beats require different tones delivered separately to left
  and right ears. A mono 1.5 Hz signal is not a valid binaural-beat
  implementation.
- Explain that binaural sessions require stereo separation and
  headphones.
- Do not claim binaural beats reliably produce a specific brain state,
  mood or performance outcome.
- Test sample rate, clipping, clicks at start/stop, continuity and
  channel output.
Editorial workflow
Jules should inventory exact current claims in UI, seed data, frequency
descriptions, programmes, articles, notifications and store-copy assets.
For each item record: exact wording, location, evidence category,
source, risk, proposed action and whether owner review is required. Do
not silently invent sources or rewrite high-risk claims without showing
the owner.
7. Optional spoken affirmations and layered audio
Product hypothesis and UX
Affirmations may make the experience feel more personal, but may
distract listeners who want sound only. Keep them optional; initially
off by default unless research supports another default.
- Let users preview the voice and type of wording.
- Provide a clear sound-only mode.
- If mixing is supported, expose separate controls for primary sound,
  ambience and speech.
- Allow skip/stop for speech without unexpectedly stopping the primary
  sound.
- Use compassionate, non-coercive wording; avoid guarantees of wealth,
  medical recovery or transformation.
- Do not claim that a particular affirmation works better at 528 Hz,
  639 Hz or another frequency without evidence.
- Do not present affirmations as treatment for mental-health
  conditions.
Technical proof-of-concept gate
Before producing a large library, Jules must inspect the actual Expo
SDK, audio packages and native configuration, then test one primary
sound plus one short spoken clip on real iOS and Android devices.
Test: 1. simultaneous playback and independent volume; 2.
play/pause/stop and fade transitions; 3. whether speech needs ducking
and whether transitions are smooth; 4. interruption by calls/other
audio; 5. app backgrounding, device lock and headphone disconnection; 6.
navigation/repeated taps and duplicate-stream prevention; 7. voice
availability, language and offline constraints; 8. exact device, OS and
package versions.
Do not assume two independent player components will mix correctly. Do
not treat a fixed "minus 5 dB" ducking value as proven; test
intelligibility and comfort. Choose native TTS, prerecorded audio or a
hybrid only after the proof of concept.
