8. Monetisation and subscription integrity
Value hypothesis
Let free users experience the core value: a meaningful session, clear
controls and honest explanations. Potential premium value to test: -
expanded curated programmes; - saved custom mixes/advanced controls; -
additional ambience and spoken sessions; - offline access, only if
reliably implemented; - useful privacy-conscious
history/personalisation.
Do not paywall essential stop controls, basic reliability or transparent
content explanations.
Technical and policy requirements
Before changing payments, Jules must audit the current implementation.
Preserve existing architecture unless a verified defect requires change.
- The purchase provider (e.g. RevenueCat/Apple/Google system as
  applicable) is the financial source of truth.
- Entitlements should be server-synchronised; an untrusted client must
  not grant or extend premium access.
- Firestore rules must prevent client-side entitlement manipulation.
- Cancellation does not necessarily end access immediately; follow
  provider-confirmed entitlement/paid-through state unless refund,
  revocation or expiry is reported.
- Webhooks should be authenticated, idempotent, auditable and
  resilient to duplicate/out-of-order events.
- Test restore purchases, renewal, cancellation, expiry,
  refund/revocation and entitlement refresh.
- Explain price, billing period, trial, renewal and cancellation
  clearly.
- Do not deploy or change live payment configuration without approval.
Do not use previously suggested prices or conversion targets as
forecasts. Test pricing and package descriptions against actual cohort
retention, refunds, cancellations and user feedback.
9. Privacy and data handling
- Collect only data needed for the feature.
- Avoid health diagnoses, detailed mental-health state, journals or
  sensitive notes unless there is a compelling need and appropriate
  safeguards.
- Never send affirmation text or private notes to analytics.
- Explain local storage versus Firebase sync.
- Review analytics, logs, crash reports and third-party SDKs for
  accidental data leakage.
- For user recordings, notes, affirmations or saved mixes, inspect
  existing data models and rules before adding paths. Enforce
  ownership, payload limits, authorised admin writes and separation of
  public content from private user content.
- Do not implement guessed Firestore schemas or broadly readable
  upload paths.
- Document deletion, retention and recovery behaviour.
Potential minimal events: session_started, session_completed,
favourite_added, mix_saved, affirmations_enabled,
affirmation_skipped, playback_error, paywall_viewed and
provider-confirmed purchase/entitlement events. Use minimal
non-sensitive properties; no raw free text.
10. Marketing and distribution
Create separate, truthful entry points for: - frequency/tone
exploration; - binaural beats and stereo listening; - sleep wind-down; -
focus/study; - relaxation/meditation; - optional spoken affirmations.
Each campaign must lead to an in-app experience that fulfils its
promise. Avoid "heals," "repairs DNA," "eliminates anxiety," "guarantees
deep sleep" and similar claims.
Low-cost plan: 1. Publish concise, evidence-aware pages explaining
tones, binaural beats, headphone requirements and session design. 2. Use
actual product demonstrations; do not depict features that are not
implemented. 3. Run small creator tests with relevant audiences and
clear sponsorship disclosures. 4. Test store-page screenshots and copy.
Apple provides Product Page
Optimisation
and Custom Product
Pages;
verify current eligibility and limits. 5. Measure activated and retained
users, not downloads alone.
Track: store impression → page visit → install → first session → first
completion → repeat session → D7/D30 return → trial/purchase → retained
paid user. Establish baseline first; do not invent universal targets.
11. Analytics and experiments
Core metrics
- Activation: new users who start and complete a first session.
- Time to value: time from opening app to first successful sound.
- Reliability: playback errors, duplicate streams, crashes and
  interruption failures.
- Retention: D1/D7/D30 return with consistent definitions.
- Repeat value: repeat sessions and favourites.
- Feature adoption: intention discovery, saved mixes, affirmation
  opt-in/skip.
- Commercial quality: trial starts, conversion, refunds,
  cancellations and paid retention by cohort.
- Trust: support complaints, claim confusion and qualitative
  feedback.
Do not optimise raw listening minutes alone; longer playback is not
automatically a better outcome.
Experiments
A. Home-screen entry - A: intention-first; B: frequency-first. -
Primary metric: successful first session. - Guardrails: time to first
sound, early exits, errors and comprehension.
B. Affirmation discovery - A: optional control on a session; B: a
dedicated optional step. - Primary metrics: opt-in and session
completion. - Guardrails: rapid disable/skip, complaints and sound-only
usage.
C. Free value - Compare simple free-content packages without
restricting essential controls. - Primary metrics: repeat sessions and
retained paid conversion. - Guardrails: cancellations, refunds, negative
feedback and first-session abandonment.
D. Store-page message - Compare an intention-led message with a
frequency-personalisation message. - Primary metrics: install conversion
and downstream first-session completion. - Guardrail: do not increase
installs with a promise the app cannot fulfil.
Predefine each hypothesis, primary metric, observation period and
decision rule. Do not claim a conclusive result from an inadequate
sample.
12. Proposed 90-day roadmap
Days 1--10: audit and baseline
- Inspect branch, dependencies, Expo configuration and tests.
- Confirm Firebase project IDs across client, rules and Functions.
- Audit audio engine, stereo/binaural implementation, interruptions
  and background behaviour.
- Audit content models, seed behaviour, admin workflow, cache/offline
  behaviour and entitlement architecture.
- Inventory high-risk claims and privacy-sensitive data paths.
- Run tests and document exact results.
- Test builds on representative real devices where available.
Deliverables: - docs/HARMONY_FREQUENCY_CURRENT_STATE_AUDIT.md -
docs/HARMONY_FREQUENCY_CLAIMS_INVENTORY.md -
docs/HARMONY_FREQUENCY_TEST_BASELINE.md
Days 11--30: core player reliability
Fix only verified, prioritised gaps. Improve playback state, stop/start,
timer, fade, interruptions, duplicate-stream prevention, accessibility
and error handling. Confirm admin-managed content is not overwritten by
seed routines.
Acceptance: core tests pass; manual device matrix is complete; no
critical audio, entitlement or Firebase regression; user can start/stop
clearly; tested flows contain no unsupported efficacy claims.
Days 31--50: validate differentiation
Prototype intention-first discovery and, only after audio audit, one
primary sound plus optional ambience/speech. Run small usability
sessions and compare entry routes.
Acceptance: feature behaviour documented; optional layers remain
optional; no duplicate streams or unexpected stops; no guaranteed
physiological claims; product owner approves expansion.
Days 51--70: monetisation/acquisition experiments
Test free/premium boundaries, purchase restore and entitlement edge
cases. Test truthful store-page variants and a small number of relevant
acquisition channels. Measure activation/retention.
Acceptance: purchase regression tests pass; terms are clear;
marketing matches the app; analytics exclude sensitive text; experiments
have defined metrics.
Days 71--90: review and scale only what works
Review activation, retention, playback reliability, paid cohorts,
affirmation opt-in/skip and user feedback. Interview active/lapsed users
where feasible. Stop or revise features that add complexity without
evidence of value.
Deliverable: docs/HARMONY_FREQUENCY_90_DAY_LEARNING_REVIEW.md, a
ranked next-quarter backlog and a scale/revise/stop decision for each
experiment.
